now we hear a lot about the metaverse  but my next guest heads a business that  claims to have been providing a bridge  between the physical and digital worlds  for the last 37 years oxford metrics  developed software supporting motion  measurement and helping infrastructure  owners such as highways authorities  manage their assets today the company  reported an 89 rise in pre-tax profits  to 4.8 million pounds for the full year  to the end of september the chief  executive of oxford metrics is nick  bolton and he joins me now nick very  good morning to you which are the parts  of the business where you've seen most  growth in demand lately  so  over the past 12 months um it's really  been in the area of virtual production  so we sell some technology that's used  uh for creating great shows like the  mandalorian you know the the disney ilm  star wars spin out and what we've seen  over the pandemic is actually  a retooling going on inside that  industry towards virtual production uh  techniques and what what that means is  that when originally it came about  because we couldn't get so many people  on set because of you know constraints  of population of vehicle density and so  forth and so they invented new ways then  to actually try to create these these  new shots with these led walls and  tracking cameras and that now has  actually become a trend that's  absolutely stuck and now there's a  fundamental retailing that's going on  inside those big tv and big big film  productions  and also  and certainly computer games so uh i  don't know if you're a video gamer but  the playstation 5 i'm sure you know has  been certainly been released and that  always strives and when those companies  come out with the  those new platforms they always drive an  upgrade cycle within our customer waste  and you know we've always had a very  strong presence in games so you know all  10 of the world's top 10 largest games  customers game companies are our  customers so as those constants come out  they do also help drive our business so  you know very much there's been we feel  over the past uh 12 months we you know  past two years with the pandemic we've  seen an acceleration of this coming of  the thing we call the augmented age  right which is this this partnership  where humans and machines are combined  to achieve more than anything can  achieve alone and the pandemics only  accelerated that interface between that  real world the virtual world that you  you know you mentioned in your  introduction you mentioned some of those  computer games companies as your  customers do you ever worry that they  might take some of the expertise that  you provide for them in-house and  develop it themselves  uh well they're very much focused on  creating those amazing games right so  their focus is in how do you create a an  engaging game uh experience that's all  about content that's about narrative and  so forth but they're still going to rely  on fundamental technology to be able to  capture the movements of the actors or  the of the athletes if they're working  on fifa soccer and so forth and that  really is where we come in so you know  we've been working in the game space uh  since 95 and so if if there was really  going to be a focus for them to sort of  come and try and implement the stuff  that we've already got i i think they  might have done it already by now rather  we see them as our very strong partners  and we're using our tools to then help  both guide what we need to develop in  the future but also help them realize  the amazing games that people get to  play these days now the other strand of  the business of course is infrastructure  management i think something like a  third of the uk's road network is  managed using your software does that  mean you're the people who are helping  implement smart motorways  uh so the software is is is an asset  management tool so it might be used to  manage certainly the roadways we all  drive on but also even the litter bins  and the emptying those litter bins uh  the lighting columns so you say you know  a third of the road network is our  customers are using it to manage about a  third of the uk's road network there's  actually half the uk streetlights are  being managed in our software uh as well  so it doesn't really matter regardless  of the asset type you know that can  still be managed in that in our in our  software and smart motorways is  certainly an element that is being uh  that is an area of attention for  managing uh assets as it would be you  know bournemouth high street uh as well  now you've talked a lot about what you  call smart sensing which are the great  untapped markets for for greater use of  sensors and monitoring  yeah i mean you're seeing it anyway you  know in all of our daily lives you can  buy an apple air tag which is a rather  nice encapsulation of smart sensing  that's one of those tags that you put in  your  on your key fob to make sure if you ever  lose your keys you can go to an app on  your phone and say where where are my  keys and it will identify you know where  it is so those sensors just as there are  sensors in my running watch that's  monitoring my heart rate or there are  sensors in our phone that world really  of of cheap available sensors coupled  with powerful analytical software that  can exist both on the device and in the  cloud is already present in in lots of  lives and i'm not saying that we're  going to dominate the world in all of  those areas but i think we have to look  to where  we we've got capability and heritage and  where our customer base is and that  broadly fits into four specific vertical  markets inside healthcare  inside engineering inside um  entertainment and inside research in all  those areas that's really where we've  been strong and so within those you know  they're all seeing those smart sensing  solutions being taken up so in games  it's about producing the gates but it's  also the way you experience uh those  those games um inside engineering it's  about  tools that enable an engineer to better  devise a new device or a new capability  so we've got a lot of the big tech  companies using using our tools i think  healthcare's perhaps the one that's  that's the most interesting um because  now you you we've you've got a way to  actually collect sensed information  about our health about our level of  activity and how idle are we when we sit  at our desk or what our range of motion  of our knee joint post um some surgery  that we've had because we had a bad  skiing accident and so forth effectively  instrumenting the quantified cell  instrumenting the self yeah in order to  be able to get data but actually you can  gather on a mass basis that researchers  can then look at and then actually make  better interventions with those people  because you've got that larger data set  i think that's probably for me the most  exciting area that i think can make a  real difference both to our business but  also actually the january to to our you  know our to our human condition to our  fellow people's lives that's a good  thing  now you mentioned data there obviously i  imagine you're collecting masses and  masses of data every day  who owns that data is it you or your  customers  it's really all of our customers so in  other words we we provide the tools by  which they gather uh data so if it's  someone like um nike or electronic arts  ea who are making a video game or trying  to understand an athlete they they buy  our system uh it gathers the data that's  stored locally then of course that's  then up to nike or ea or mit or nasa to  best manage that data on the healthcare  side clearly rightly so there are much  they're very clear constraints about  what you can do with patient information  and patient data and again that means  it's the hospital the research institute  who owns that  and then they manage all of those things  so although i would say our systems are  gathering a great deal of data  that doesn't come to us that was all  held locally by the uh by the individual  operator

